issue.body:
### Orca version

0.5.3 (installed from npm) and 0.5.8 (latest release, run with `npx -y @blade-ai/orca@0.5.8`). The assistant arms of `emit_surface_event` quoted below are identical in `v0.5.3` and `v0.5.8`.

### Installation method

Package manager

### Platform

macOS, Apple Silicon (arm64), Node v22.23.1

### Mode

Other: `orca --mode=acp` (ACP over stdio, not a shared session). Found through an ACP client ([Pilion](https://github.com/echoVic/pilion-browser)), reproduced with the plain Node client below.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Save the script below as `orca-acp-repro.mjs`. It spawns `orca --mode=acp`, sends one read-only prompt in a temp directory, and merges the `agent_message_chunk` updates the way a client renders them.
2. Run `node orca-acp-repro.mjs`, or `ORCA_CMD="npx -y @blade-ai/orca@0.5.8" node orca-acp-repro.mjs`. It exits with code 1 when the last chunk is a verbatim copy of the text already streamed.

<details>
<summary>orca-acp-repro.mjs</summary>

```js
#!/usr/bin/env node
// Repro: `orca --mode=acp` streams the final answer as many small agent_message_chunk updates,
// then sends the complete answer again as one more chunk right before the turn ends.
//
//   node orca-acp-repro.mjs                                   # uses `orca` from PATH
//   ORCA_CMD="npx -y @blade-ai/orca@0.5.8" node orca-acp-repro.mjs
//
// Runs one read-only prompt in a temp directory. It needs the model credentials Orca normally uses.
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process';
import { mkdtemp, rm, writeFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';
import { join } from 'node:path';

const [command, ...commandArgs] = (process.env.ORCA_CMD ?? 'orca').split(' ');
const dir = await mkdtemp(join(tmpdir(), 'orca-acp-repro-'));
await writeFile(
  join(dir, 'hello.txt'),
  'Weekend plan\nSat: market, then sort the bookshelf.\nSun: a 5 km run, then a quiet dinner.\n',
);
const child = spawn(command, [...commandArgs, '--mode=acp'], {
  cwd: dir,
  stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'inherit'],
});
setTimeout(() => {
  console.error('timed out');
  child.kill();
  process.exit(1);
}, 120_000).unref();

let nextId = 1;
const pending = new Map();
const write = (message) => child.stdin.write(`${JSON.stringify({ jsonrpc: '2.0', ...message })}\n`);
const request = (method, params) =>
  new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const id = nextId++;
    pending.set(id, { resolve, reject });
    write({ id, method, params });
  });

const updates = [];
let buffer = '';
child.stdout.on('data', (data) => {
  buffer += data;
  for (let end; (end = buffer.indexOf('\n')) >= 0; ) {
    const line = buffer.slice(0, end);
    buffer = buffer.slice(end + 1);
    if (line.trim()) onMessage(JSON.parse(line));
  }
});
function onMessage(message) {
  if (message.method === 'session/update') updates.push(message.params);
  else if (message.method === 'session/request_permission') {
    // Only read-only tools are expected here; anything else is refused.
    const read = ['read', 'search'].includes(message.params.toolCall?.kind);
    const option = message.params.options.find((o) => o.kind === (read ? 'allow_once' : 'reject_once'));
    write({ id: message.id, result: { outcome: { outcome: 'selected', optionId: option.optionId } } });
  } else if (message.id !== undefined && pending.has(message.id)) {
    const { resolve, reject } = pending.get(message.id);
    pending.delete(message.id);
    message.error ? reject(new Error(JSON.stringify(message.error))) : resolve(message.result);
  }
}

try {
  const init = await request('initialize', {
    protocolVersion: 1,
    clientCapabilities: {},
    clientInfo: { name: 'acp-repro', version: '0' },
  });
  console.log('agent:', JSON.stringify(init.agentInfo));
  const { sessionId } = await request('session/new', { cwd: dir, mcpServers: [] });
  const prompt =
    'Read hello.txt in the current directory, then summarize it in 3-4 sentences and give it a short title.';
  const result = await request('session/prompt', {
    sessionId,
    prompt: [{ type: 'text', text: prompt }],
  });
  console.log('stopReason:', result.stopReason);

  // Merge consecutive agent_message_chunk updates the way a client renders them (a tool call starts a new message).
  const messages = [];
  let current;
  for (const params of updates) {
    const { update } = params;
    if (update.sessionUpdate === 'agent_message_chunk' && update.content.type === 'text') {
      if (!current) messages.push((current = []));
      current.push({ text: update.content.text, meta: params._meta });
    } else if (update.sessionUpdate === 'tool_call') current = undefined;
  }
  const final = messages.at(-1);
  const last = final.at(-1);
  const streamed = final.slice(0, -1).map((c) => c.text).join('');
  console.log(`final message: ${final.length} chunks, sizes of the last 4: ${final.slice(-4).map((c) => c.text.length)}`);
  console.log('_meta of a streamed chunk:', JSON.stringify(final.at(-2).meta));
  console.log('_meta of the last chunk  :', JSON.stringify(last.meta));
  const duplicated = last.text.length > 20 && streamed.endsWith(last.text);
  console.log(`last chunk (${last.text.length} chars) is a verbatim copy of what was already streamed: ${duplicated}`);
  console.log('--- final message as a client would show it ---');
  console.log(final.map((c) => c.text).join(''));
  process.exitCode = duplicated ? 1 : 0;
} finally {
  child.kill();
  await rm(dir, { recursive: true, force: true });
}
```

</details>

### Expected behavior

The completed answer reaches the client exactly once. After the deltas have been streamed, `ResponseCompleted` should add only text that has not been sent yet, which is nothing when the stream was complete. The shared-session observer already does this: it remembers how much of each stream it sent, sends `item.text.get(offset..)` and attaches `_meta { itemId, offset }` ([observer.rs L257-L274](https://github.com/echoVic/orca-agent/blob/bef73ea292f61885a2be66a4976e7e9345160e4e/crates/orca-runtime/src/acp/observer.rs#L257-L274)).

### Actual behavior

In plain `--mode=acp`, the final answer is streamed as many small `agent_message_chunk` updates, and then the **whole answer is sent again as one more `agent_message_chunk`** just before the turn ends. A client that appends chunks (which is what ACP defines) shows the answer twice. No `_meta` is attached to the chunks: the script prints it for one streamed chunk and for the last one, and both are empty.

Output of the script on 0.5.3:

```
agent: {"name":"orca","title":"Orca","version":"0.5.3"}
stopReason: end_turn
final message: 86 chunks, sizes of the last 4: 4,4,2,284
_meta of a streamed chunk: undefined
_meta of the last chunk  : undefined
last chunk (284 chars) is a verbatim copy of what was already streamed: true
--- final message as a client would show it ---
The file is a short weekend plan. Let me summarize in 3-4 sentences with a short title.**Title: Weekend Plan**

The file lays out a two-day weekend schedule. Saturday starts with a trip to the market, followed by sorting the bookshelf. Sunday features a 5 km run and ends with a quiet dinner. The overall tone is simple and relaxed, balancing errands, exercise, and rest.**Title: Weekend Plan**

The file lays out a two-day weekend schedule. Saturday starts with a trip to the market, followed by sorting the bookshelf. Sunday features a 5 km run and ends with a quiet dinner. The overall tone is simple and relaxed, balancing errands, exercise, and rest.
```

Output on 0.5.8:

```
agent: {"name":"orca","title":"Orca","version":"0.5.8"}
stopReason: end_turn
final message: 172 chunks, sizes of the last 4: 4,9,1,355
_meta of a streamed chunk: undefined
_meta of the last chunk  : undefined
last chunk (355 chars) is a verbatim copy of what was already streamed: true
--- final message as a client would show it ---
The file is very short. Summarize in 3-4 sentences and give a short title. The content: Weekend plan — Saturday: market, then sort the bookshelf. Sunday: a 5 km run, then a quiet dinner.

I need to summarize in 3-4 sentences. That's a bit odd for such a short file, but I'll comply: 3-4 sentences. Let me write a title and 3-4 sentences.**Title: A Simple Weekend Plan**

The note lays out a simple weekend plan with one activity block for each day. Saturday starts with a trip to the market, followed by sorting the bookshelf. Sunday is more active, beginning with a 5 km run and winding down with a quiet dinner. Overall, it balances errands and light exercise with calm, unhurried downtime.**Title: A Simple Weekend Plan**

The note lays out a simple weekend plan with one activity block for each day. Saturday starts with a trip to the market, followed by sorting the bookshelf. Sunday is more active, beginning with a 5 km run and winding down with a quiet dinner. Overall, it balances errands and light exercise with calm, unhurried downtime.
```

**Where it comes from.** The plain path is `drain_surface_prompt` ([L2406](https://github.com/echoVic/orca-agent/blob/bef73ea292f61885a2be66a4976e7e9345160e4e/crates/orca-runtime/src/acp/agent.rs#L2406)) → `project_surface_event` ([L2800](https://github.com/echoVic/orca-agent/blob/bef73ea292f61885a2be66a4976e7e9345160e4e/crates/orca-runtime/src/acp/agent.rs#L2800)) → `emit_surface_event` ([L2706](https://github.com/echoVic/orca-agent/blob/bef73ea292f61885a2be66a4976e7e9345160e4e/crates/orca-runtime/src/acp/agent.rs#L2706)). Its two assistant arms ([L2713-L2724](https://github.com/echoVic/orca-agent/blob/bef73ea292f61885a2be66a4976e7e9345160e4e/crates/orca-runtime/src/acp/agent.rs#L2713-L2724)) are:

- `AssistantPatch::Delta { text, .. }` becomes `AgentMessageChunk(text)` for every delta;
- `AssistantPatch::ResponseCompleted { response }` becomes `AgentMessageChunk(item.text)` with the complete `message_item.text`, with no knowledge of what was already streamed.

In the runs I looked at, text streamed before a tool call was not duplicated; only the final message of the turn was. In my own Pilion history the doubled final answers only occur in Orca conversations.

**Same function, second symptom: reasoning arrives as message text.** The `Delta` arm above destructures `{ text, .. }` and `agent.rs` never looks at a stream's channel, so reasoning and plan deltas are also sent as `agent_message_chunk`. The observer path maps `AssistantChannel::Reasoning` to `agent_thought_chunk` ([observer.rs L476-L492](https://github.com/echoVic/orca-agent/blob/bef73ea292f61885a2be66a4976e7e9345160e4e/crates/orca-runtime/src/acp/observer.rs#L476-L492)). In the 0.5.8 output above, the model's reasoning ("I need to summarize in 3-4 sentences. That's a bit odd for such a short file, but I'll comply…") appears at the start of the final message as ordinary text.

**Suggested fix.** Give the plain path the same per-stream bookkeeping as the observer: remember each stream's channel and how much of it was sent, send only the remaining suffix on `ResponseCompleted`, and send reasoning as `agent_thought_chunk`. A regression test for `crates/orca-runtime/tests/acp_agent.rs`: a turn made of deltas plus `ResponseCompleted` yields exactly one copy of the message text on the wire, and no `agent_message_chunk` for reasoning.

### Regression details

Not known to be a regression. The `emit_surface_event` assistant arms are identical in v0.5.3 and v0.5.8; I did not test older versions.

### Logs and diagnostics

See the two outputs above. The prompt and the file are synthetic, so nothing in this report comes from a real project.

